<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Namdhari</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kuka movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Namdharis, also called Kukas, are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> sect of the 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, given its form by Satguru Ram Singh from 1857. They stress simple living, vegetarianism and the recitation of God's name, and they boycotted British goods and institutions. After a Kuka attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malerkotla">Malerkotla</a> in 1872 the British executed 65 of them by blowing them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannons</a>, and Ram Singh was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p></div>
